{"id": "EUVD-2026-86080", "enisaUuid": "e684a23c-849a-3270-bc20-3d79150870a9", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nusb: gadget: goku_udc: avoid NULL deref of dev->driver in INT_USBRESET log\n\ngoku_irq() handles a number of bus events under a single ep0 path.\nIt already guards the gadget driver suspend/resume callbacks against a\nNULL ->driver:\n\n\tif (dev->gadget.speed != USB_SPEED_UNKNOWN\n\t\t\t&& dev->driver\n\t\t\t&& dev->driver->resume) {\n\t\tspin_unlock(&dev->lock);\n\t\tdev->driver->resume(&dev->gadget);\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\nbut the very next branch unconditionally dereferences dev->driver\nwhen an INT_USBRESET arrives:\n\n\tif (stat & INT_USBRESET) {\n\t\tACK(INT_USBRESET);\n\t\tINFO(dev, \"USB reset done, gadget %s\\n\",\n\t\t\tdev->driver->driver.name);\n\t}\n\nIf the controller raises INT_USBRESET before any gadget driver has\nbeen bound (or after one has been unbound), dev->driver is NULL and\nthe printk dereferences NULL.\n\nsmatch flags the inconsistency:\n\n  drivers/usb/gadget/udc/goku_udc.c:1618 goku_irq() error:\n  we previously assumed 'dev->driver' could be null (see line 1607)\n\nFall back to a placeholder when the gadget driver is not bound.\n\nNo functional change while a gadget driver is bound.", "datePublished": "2026-09-24T16:04:25", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-03T10:58:46", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/d7b33afbf9b54b61cb423af9663df54eae11d3b1", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/ea2901a4a90afc662e4d023542d91f10ab10fd3c", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/999ff3bf665f2abfb6af519ef3d6d42b061d3c15", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a018d4195114ca2cf79858cc45c98581dee4795b", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/10d7fc3008d5b7ec03af8e08927f1d090014c2af", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/28d78783a871cdb7a637dcb72452ff7d303430ae", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/5bf5e3fba9bc7dfd69701521dbe9809f8ccbdb02"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-97482", "GHSA-hc2c-96rf-cqvj"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.17, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "06de0fd5-aede-3d85-a5d0-f5f1774802d4", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "07337f0a-849a-33ce-95a7-ab3f0bbdfc88", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "1da177e4c3f41524e886b7f1b8a0c1fc7321cac2 <5bf5e3fba9bc7dfd69701521dbe9809f8ccbdb02"}, {"id": "1da109b9-abec-3b26-91a2-0b38a487ca55", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.53"}, {"id": "1e4091ac-a965-3170-ae54-f9137ce68066", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "1da177e4c3f41524e886b7f1b8a0c1fc7321cac2 <28d78783a871cdb7a637dcb72452ff7d303430ae"}, {"id": "1e8b0e5f-c8d9-30ab-b91f-acf93ee23896", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "1da177e4c3f41524e886b7f1b8a0c1fc7321cac2 <10d7fc3008d5b7ec03af8e08927f1d090014c2af"}, {"id": "26aa0ae9-171c-32c8-9c55-5663b150db89", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.10.271"}, {"id": "316d5e7f-f90c-308a-a05f-42fe0e4642db", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "1da177e4c3f41524e886b7f1b8a0c1fc7321cac2 <d7b33afbf9b54b61cb423af9663df54eae11d3b1"}, {"id": "59a1dd57-e69d-3c9e-a17b-fd049b44be18", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.1.189"}, {"id": "6105019f-cc3d-3db4-a8a3-42bc4eeb9c57", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.111"}, {"id": "783fb418-fec1-3175-a353-fdf02330886a", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.158"}, {"id": "95e035ea-7caf-37ec-b498-ab6cd0193982", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "2.6.12"}, {"id": "a5907a9e-225e-365d-8f1c-3c5921fc481f", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "produc